.TH SYSCONF 2P "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W POSIX 1003.1
.SH NAME
sysconf \- (POSIX only) get configurable system variables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <unistd.h>
.PP
.ft B
long sysconf(int \fIname\fR);
.ft R
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The information in this specification applies
only to POSIX applications.
.PP
The
.IR sysconf 
function provides a method for an application to determined the current
value of a configurable system limit or option.
.PP
The
.I name 
argument represents the system variable to be queried.  The
following variables are supported:
.PP
.bd I 3
.RS
.TP 35
\fBVariable\fP
\fIname\fP \fBValue\fP			
.TP 35
{ARG_MAX}
{_SC_ARG_MAX}		
.TP 35
{CHILD_MAX}
{_SC_CHILD_MAX}		
.TP 35
{clock ticks/second}
{_SC_CLK_TCK}	
.TP 35
{NGROUPS_MAX}
{_SC_NGROUPS_MAX}		
.TP 35
{OPEN_MAX}
{_SC_OPEN_MAX}
.TP 35
{STREAM_BUF}
{_SC_STREAM_BUF}
.TP 35
{TZNAME_MAX}
{_SC_TZNAME_MAX}
.TP 35
{_POSIX_JOB_CONTROL}
{_SC_JOB_CONTROL}
.TP 35
{_POSIX_SAVED_IDS}
{_SC_SAVED_IDS}
.TP 35
{_POSIX_VERSION}
{_SC_VERSION}
.RE
.bd I
.IP
.PP
The meaning of each variable is as follows:
.PP
.RS
.TP 35
\fBVariable\fP
\fBDescription\fP
.TP 35
{ARG_MAX}
Maximum length of all arguments + environment strings to \fIexec()\fR.
.TP 35
{CHILD_MAX}
Maximum number of simultaneous processes per real user ID.
.TP 35
{clock ticks/second}
The number of clock ticks per second.
.TP 35
{NGROUPS_MAX}
Maximum number of simultaneous supplementary group IDs per process.
.TP 35
{OPEN_MAX}
Maximum number of simultaneous open files per process.
.TP 35
{STREAM_BUF}
The number of streams that one process can have open at one time.
.TP 35
{TZNAME_MAX}
The maximum number of bytes supported for the name of a time zone.
.TP 35
{_POSIX_JOB_CONTROL}
If defined, the system supports POSIX job control.
.TP 35
{_POSIX_SAVED_IDS}
If defined, saved set-user-IDs and set-group-IDS are supported.
.TP 35
{_POSIX_VERSION}
The version of POSIX supported by the system.
.RE
.RE
.PP
The values for the variables above are given in the headers 
<limits.h> or <unistd.h>.  The symbolic constants used as values for
.I name
are given in <unistd.h>.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Upon successful completion 
.IR sysconf 
returns the current variable value on the system.  If 
.I name
is an invalid value, 
.IR sysconf 
returns \-1.  If the variable corresponding to
.I name
is associated with functionality that is not supported by the system,
.IR sysconf 
shall return \-1 without changing the value of 
.I errno.
.SH "ERRORS"
If any of the following conditions occur, 
.IR sysconf
returns \-1 and set \fIerrno\fP to the corresponding value:
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
The value of \fIname\fP is invalid.
